0.01013946533203125, 0.01116180419921875, -0.00925445556640625, 0.0111846923828125, 0.0170135498046875, 0.021209716796875, 0.02288818359375, 0.003082275390625, -0.0179443359375, -0.016937255859375, -0.011871337890625, -0.006870269775390625, 0.0223541259765625, -0.003082275390625, -0.01528167724609375, 0.009368896484375, -0.026336669921875, -0.0273284912109375, 0.0313720703125, -0.0240325927734375, 0.053558349609375, 0.024566650390625, 0.02203369140625, 0.02978515625, -0.04400634765625, -0.017486572265625, 0.0311126708984375, -0.01126861572265625, -0.008514404296875, -0.005237579345703125, 0.005573272705078125, 0.01004791259765625, -0.044830322265625, 0.0093841552734375, -0.00733184814453125, -0.005764007568359375, -0.00414276123046875, 0.044830322265625, -0.00812530517578125, -0.0148468017578125, -0.0203094482421875, -0.00537872314453125, 0.00901031494140625, -0.017120361328125, -0.0018463134765625, 0.00778961181640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "A volcano is a vent or fissure in the crust of a planetary-mass object that allows hot lava, volcanic ash, and gases to escape from a magma chamber\u2014a reservoir of molten rock\u2014below the surface. This document provides an overview of the main types of volcanoes on Earth, their tectonic settings, and the geological processes that lead to their formation. Specifically, it addresses volcanoes at plate boundaries (divergent, convergent, and transform), volcanism in rift zones, intraplate volcanism and hotspots, and the principal types of volcanoes found on Earth.\n\n**Main Types of Volcanoes**\n\nVolcanoes are commonly classified into three main types based on their shape, eruption style, and composition:\n- **Shield volcanoes** have broad, gently sloping sides and are built by the eruption of low-viscosity basaltic lava. The Hawaiian Islands are classic examples.\n- **Stratovolcanoes** (or composite volcanoes) are large, steep-sided volcanoes formed by alternating layers of lava flows, ash, and volcanic rocks. They are often associated with explosive eruptions, as seen in Mount Fuji and Mount St. Helens.\n- **Cinder cone volcanoes** are the smallest type, consisting of steep, conical hills of volcanic fragments (cinders) that accumulate around a single vent. Par\u00edcutin in Mexico is a well-known example.\n\n**Volcanoes at Plate Boundaries**\n\nOn Earth, volcanoes are most commonly associated with tectonic plate boundaries. The majority of Earth's plate boundaries are underwater, and it is estimated that about 80% of the world\u2019s active volcanoes are found beneath the oceans, particularly along mid-ocean ridges (International Association of Volcanology and Chemistry of the Earth's Interior [IAVCEI], https://www.iavceivolcano.org/). For example, the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is a site of divergent plate boundaries, where tectonic plates move apart and magma rises to create new crust. At these divergent boundaries, magma is typically basaltic\u2014low in silica and dissolved gas\u2014which results in relatively gentle, non-explosive eruptions.\n\nIn contrast, convergent plate boundaries occur where plates move toward each other, often resulting in one plate being forced beneath another in a process called subduction. This process leads to the formation of volcanoes that are often highly explosive, as seen in the Pacific Ring of Fire. At convergent boundaries, the subducting plate carries water and sediments into the mantle, which lowers the melting point of the overlying mantle wedge (the region of mantle above the subducting plate) and produces magma that is richer in silica and gas. This magma is more viscous and traps gases more easily, leading to explosive eruptions, in contrast to the gentler eruptions at divergent boundaries.\n\n**Volcanoes in Rift Zones**\n\nVolcanoes can also form in regions where the Earth's crust is stretching and thinning, even if these areas are not located at plate boundaries. Examples include the East African Rift, where the African plate is splitting into two and creating a zone of active volcanism; the Wells Gray-Clearwater volcanic field in Canada, associated with continental rifting and crustal extension; and the Rio Grande rift in North America. While all these rift zones involve crustal thinning that allows magma to reach the surface, the style of volcanism can differ: the East African Rift features large shield volcanoes and fissure eruptions, while the Wells Gray-Clearwater field is characterized by smaller cinder cones and lava flows, and the Rio Grande rift displays a mix of volcanic types. These differences are influenced by local geology, magma composition, and the extent of crustal extension.\n\n**Intraplate Volcanism and Hotspots**\n\nMoving away from plate boundaries, some volcanoes form through intraplate volcanism. This is typically attributed to mantle plumes\u2014columns of hot, buoyant rock rising from deep within the mantle, possibly originating near the core\u2013mantle boundary or the lower mantle. When a mantle plume reaches the base of the lithosphere (the rigid outer layer of the Earth), it can cause localized melting and create a hotspot, which is a region of persistent volcanic activity not directly associated with plate boundaries. As a tectonic plate moves over a stationary hotspot, a chain of volcanoes can form, such as the Hawaiian Islands. While the mantle plume theory is widely accepted, alternative explanations for hotspots include shallow mantle convection or lithospheric extension, which propose that hotspots may result from processes in the upper mantle or stretching of the lithosphere rather than deep mantle plumes. However, mantle plumes remain the most common model.\n\n**Transform Boundaries and Volcanism**\n\nIn contrast to the settings described above, volcanoes are usually not created at transform boundaries, where two tectonic plates slide past one another horizontally. Transform boundaries, such as the San Andreas Fault, do not typically provide a pathway for magma to reach the surface because there is little crustal thinning or subduction to generate melt. As a result, volcanic activity is rare in these settings, distinguishing transform boundaries from divergent and convergent boundaries where volcanism is much more common.\n\nIn summary, volcanoes are diverse in form and origin, with their characteristics and eruption styles closely tied to their tectonic settings and the geological processes at work beneath the surface. Understanding these relationships helps explain the distribution and behavior of volcanoes across the planet."}}